 The application is developed in Unity 6 (6000.3.10f1) using the Unity OpenXR Plugin (1.16.1), HTC VIVE OpenXR integration (2.5.1), and Unity XR Interaction Toolkit (3.3.1). It is deployed as a standalone Android ARM64 application on the HTC VIVE Focus Vision head-mounted display, with rendering performed locally on the headset using the Universal Render Pipeline (17.3.0). The study uses the headset's virtual-reality configuration, in which the headset supplies head-pose and eye-gaze tracking and the tracked right-hand controller supplies pointing and trigger input. Network access supports cloud-based agent decisions and voice preparation; it is not used to stream rendered frames from a host computer. HTC specifies 2448 $\times$ 2448 pixels per eye, a 90-Hz standalone display refresh rate, and a field of view of up to 120 degrees~\cite{htc2026focusvision}. The separately advertised 120-Hz binocular gaze output is a sensor specification, not proof of the application's effective acquisition rate. Actual rendering and gaze-delivery rates require measurement on the study build.
 
