+The search task takes place in a neutral virtual room. Seated participants interact using the right controller, while colored geometric objects hang on eight vertical planes arranged around them; Figure~\ref{fig:scene} shows the layout from above. Because the application knows the target identity but withholds it from the opening instructions, participants must rely on voice guidance to locate the target, which they select by pointing the controller ray at it and pressing the trigger. The current stimulus generator includes six shapes---sphere, cube, pyramid, cylinder, capsule, and star---in red, blue, yellow, and green. Eight vertical planes surround the seated origin at 45-degree intervals. The current development configuration contains 168 objects, or 21 per plane, at a default plane radius of 1.5~m from the seated origin. These object counts are separate from the eight target trials assigned to each absolute nominal angle category in a 40-trial block. Objects vary in height and horizontal position within their assigned plane, so search requires both coarse orientation toward a plane and finer search within it. For each trial, the system logs object positions, layout seed, wall assignment, and target identity.
+
+The October 23, 2026 layout revision reduces the full wall height by 30\%, from 3.148~m to 2.2036~m (approximately 2.20~m). Object centers occupy heights from 0.10~m to 2.1036~m above the floor, leaving a 0.10-m margin above the highest permitted center. The generator redistributes the same number of objects within this shorter vertical range while retaining the horizontal bounds, object sizes, and minimum three-dimensional center-to-center separation of 0.20~m. Thus, the denser arrangement results from vertical compaction rather than additional objects or smaller shapes. Layouts are generated deterministically from a saved seed within the revised bounds; earlier layouts are not simply scaled downward.
+
+Both voice conditions use the same layout version and spatial constraints. The shorter walls are an implemented design change, not evidence of improved comfort or search performance. Seated-comfort, visibility, ray-selection, and realized height-allocation checks remain necessary before these parameters are frozen for participant collection.
 
