 \subsection{Frame-Level Records and the Condensed Trial CSV}
 The recording separates time-varying observations from trial summaries. Frame records retain application time, gaze origin and direction with tracking validity, head pose, available controller poses and input state, and the gaze/controller object-intersection evidence used by guidance. Object identities and transforms provide the scene needed for reconstruction; guidance transitions, agent decisions, actual speech playback, trigger selections, and pauses are recorded as events. A rendered frame can reuse a sensor observation, so frame count must not be reported as the number of independent eye-tracking samples.
 
+For new rotational-search recordings, the trial replay snapshot additionally stores the wall bottom in recording-local coordinates, the wall height in metres, and a flag indicating that these bounds are available. Trial Review uses those recorded bounds to reconstruct the shorter walls. Earlier recordings without explicit bounds retain object-derived wall estimates and their original object positions; they are not rescaled to the current layout. These replay fields supplement the layout version and saved transforms and are distinct from the columns in the condensed CSV. Analyses that include recordings from both layouts must retain that provenance rather than treat the geometry as unchanged.
+
 Trial Review generates an Excel-compatible \texttt{trials\_condensed.csv} from the fuller \texttt{trials.csv}. Each compact row corresponds to a source trial row and groups columns for performance and subjective review (Table~\ref{tab:condensed-fields}). The compact file supports inspection and analysis preparation; the full export retains outcome, practice, provenance, and quality fields needed to decide which rows belong in an analysis.
 
